items.content:
🆕 Perception Agents

https://t.co/SY7T0wjZou

@anbarth joins us for the 4th year in a row to discuss why agents are -still- struggling with real "messy" knowledge work, and how @Amazon's AGI Lab is solving it through agents that can perceive, plan, and act, while open sourcing their harness and tooling along the way!

@AndrewLampinen my arguments going back to 1998 are they you can’t do human level cognition (in its broad, flexible, generalizable form) with symbolic operations SOMEWHERE in the systems, and those symbolic operations are critical. i have always left it open *where* in the system that would happen and how it would be implemented. strongly recommend you read the Algebraic Mind (2001) to understand how i have always couched the argument.

An often expressed counter argument was that you could just do thing with some sort of deep feed forward neural network. Hinton for example likened including symbols to including gasoline engines on top of electric, and called symbols “aether”. 

so far as i can tell i was correct and his arguments (and those of many others) were wrong, except in domains where you can really fully cover the training space perhaps with symbolically generated augmented data.

